    "original_title": "Bedqabka Aadanaha: Sannadaha ay Dawladi ku Suurtagelin Karto (1/2)",
    "slug": "somalia-s-unregistered-births-a-growing-crisis-for-governance",
    "summary": "With an estimated 700,000 to 800,000 births per year, Somalia's lack of a civil registration system leaves most children without official documentation, hindering development and access to basic rights.",
    "content": "Every day, an estimated 1,900 to 2,200 children are born in Somalia, a country with a population of roughly 20 million people. Over the course of a year, this amounts to a surge of 700,000 to 800,000 new citizens—a number comparable to the population of a thriving city, yet one that goes uncounted as the nation struggles with limited administrative capacity and pervasive poverty.\n\nThe scale of births highlights the absence of a functional civil registration system. Although Somalia has a population growth rate among the highest globally, the exact number of births remains unknown because the government lacks the infrastructure to record them. UN agencies, such as UNICEF, have noted the profound impact of this oversight, particularly for children under five years old, who often live without legal identity, making them vulnerable to exclusion from education, healthcare, and protection services.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this issue resonates deeply. Many diaspora families maintain ties with relatives back home, often supporting communities that are left without formal records. Understanding the birth registration deficit is crucial for diaspora-led initiatives aimed at improving social services. Without reliable data, planning for schools, health clinics, and employment programs becomes guesswork, hampering efforts to effectively allocate resources.\n\nMoreover, the lack of registration feeds into wider governance challenges. An unregistered population complicates electoral processes, property rights, and social security, undermining trust in state institutions. While the government has shown commitment to reform, the scale of the problem demands urgent attention and international cooperation. For Somalis abroad, supporting solutions—whether through advocacy or funding—could be pivotal in helping future generations gain their rightful status in a country striving to rebuild.\n\nEfforts to address this crisis require a multi-faceted approach, including community-based registration drives and the use of digital technology. As Somalia continues its path toward stability, ensuring that every child counts is not just an administrative necessity but a moral imperative for a dignified future.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/06/bedqabka-aadanaha-sannadaha-ay-dawladi-ku-suurtagelin-karto-1-2/",
